#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295769 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d806fdb4-05d2-5b37-afe9-78a9c2b53ac1" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Using Linear Equations With One Variable Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a933257-3c91-5c96-a5de-067025881fd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.03(T-25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295771}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a933257-3c91-5c96-a5de-067025881fd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.03(T-25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295771} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d7ce8df-40f6-5cc9-aee0-5a0f9d6d3380" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f23612a-4e3e-5819-aa73-367004b70fcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-168073" :question/problem "Let $T(t)$ represent the temperature of an object at time $t,$ in minutes, since the moment when it was put in a room. The temperature *decreases* at a rate that is equal to $0.03$ times the difference between the object's temperature and the room's temperature, which is ${25}^{∘}\\text{C}.$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nWhich differential equation describes the instantaneous rate of change in the object's temperature?\n\n*Hint: Write the right-hand side of the equation as an expression in terms of $T.$*\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change in the temperature $T$ of the object is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe are told that the temperature *decreases* at a rate that is equal to $0.03$ times $(T-25).$ Therefore, the corresponding differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-0.03(T-25).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295772 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff5ec0b-1456-5231-94e1-0fc5923d6672" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dd148dc-6eb6-5f0a-89c1-b5a2feb5fb81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1dd148dc-6eb6-5f0a-89c1-b5a2feb5fb81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295774} :answer-field/id #uuid "63e8041a-cbef-5c87-9712-42d3ab24bbde" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1c7842b4-b0d2-5165-961b-5966b5cd5f32" :question/math-academy-id "q-168074" :question/problem "Let $T(t)$ represent the temperature of an object at time $t,$ in minutes, since the moment when it was put in a room. The temperature *decreases* at a rate that is equal to $0.03$ times the difference between the object's temperature and the room's temperature, which is ${25}^{∘}\\text{C}.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nWhich differential equation describes the instantaneous rate of change in the object's temperature?\n\n*Hint: Write the right-hand side of the equation as an expression in terms of $T.$*\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-0.03(T-25)$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nHow fast is the object's temperature *decreasing* at the moment when its temperature is ${60}^{∘}C?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The object's temperature is decreasing at a rate of $-$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$degrees per minute." :question/worked-solution "To find the rate, we substitute $T=60$ into our equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}{|}_{T=60} & =-0.03(T-25){|}_{T=60} \\\\  & =-0.03(60-25) \\\\  & =-0.03(35) \\\\  & =-1.05\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $T={60}^{∘}C,$ the object's temperature decreases at a rate of $1.05$ degrees per minute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295775 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f28fbcc-6d9c-5dc7-89cc-13255db78765" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Second Derivative at a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42138c40-d848-58e8-b042-ac104e97663d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0009(T-25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42138c40-d848-58e8-b042-ac104e97663d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0009(T-25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295777} :answer-field/id #uuid "15a79c3a-8df9-5d7c-a21a-9495114d04ec" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe1d1c42-7578-502a-a670-1cf49da1604c" :question/math-academy-id "q-168076" :question/problem "Let $T(t)$ represent the temperature of an object at time $t,$ in minutes, since the moment when it was put in a room. The temperature *decreases* at a rate that is equal to $0.03$ times the difference between the object's temperature and the room's temperature, which is ${25}^{∘}\\text{C}.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nWhich differential equation describes the instantaneous rate of change in the object's temperature?\n\n*Hint: Write the right-hand side of the equation as an expression in terms of $T.$*\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-0.03(T-25)$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nHow fast is the object's temperature *decreasing* at the moment when its temperature is ${60}^{∘}C?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The object's temperature is decreasing at a rate of $-$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1.05$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$degrees per minute.\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}T}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}$ in terms of $T.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}T}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-0.03(T-25)) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}T}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-0.03T+0.75) \\\\  & =-0.03\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(T)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0.75) \\\\  & =-0.03\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}$ into the above and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}T}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =-0.03\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =-0.03(-0.03(T-25)) \\\\  & =0.0009(T-25)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295778 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16f0876a-33f0-5c6b-9195-8b9a0a550655" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Solution in Explicit Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9226fd8-50a0-5a85-bf43-ebb112ffaed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-0.03t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9226fd8-50a0-5a85-bf43-ebb112ffaed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-0.03t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295780} :answer-field/id #uuid "225b5a66-5dc6-5fd3-84e1-88a5c5dfd035" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295779}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "94f3bb43-15c5-5035-aaa0-448fb1039607" :question/math-academy-id "q-168077" :question/problem "Let $T(t)$ represent the temperature of an object at time $t,$ in minutes, since the moment when it was put in a room. The temperature *decreases* at a rate that is equal to $0.03$ times the difference between the object's temperature and the room's temperature, which is ${25}^{∘}\\text{C}.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nWhich differential equation describes the instantaneous rate of change in the object's temperature?\n\n*Hint: Write the right-hand side of the equation as an expression in terms of $T.$*\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-0.03(T-25)$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nHow fast is the object's temperature *decreasing* at the moment when its temperature is ${60}^{∘}C?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The object's temperature is decreasing at a rate of $-$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1.05$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$degrees per minute.\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nFind $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}T}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}$ in terms of $T.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}T}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0.0009(T-25)$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind the general solution to the differential equation in part 1 for $T\\ne 25.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $T\\,=\\,25+K\\cdot \\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\,$where $K$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we separate variables and integrate both sides with respect to $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t} & =-0.03(T-25) \\\\ \\frac{1}{T-25}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t} & =-0.03 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{T-25}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int -0.03\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}T}{T-25} & =-0.03\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-25| & =-0.03t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a perfectly valid general solution. However, if we want to write $T=T(t)$ explicitly, then we can solve for $T.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-25| & =-0.03t+C \\\\ |T-25| & ={e}^{-0.03t+C} \\\\ |T-25| & =K\\,{e}^{-0.03t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}$ is an arbitrary positive constant.\n\nWe can drop the absolute value bars by allowing $K$ to be both positive and negative. Therefore, the most general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =25+K\\,{e}^{-0.03t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295781 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "99112850-043d-53e5-8d61-fecb0317bd11" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by Separating the Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce225fea-590e-58e3-a68b-9ac26e570830" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25+3e^{-0.03t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce225fea-590e-58e3-a68b-9ac26e570830" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25+3e^{-0.03t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295783} :answer-field/id #uuid "007d16d6-09b9-5f50-a425-4af16c221476" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a4ac3eca-97f0-5631-8b10-1482c1359d57" :question/math-academy-id "q-168078" :question/problem "Let $T(t)$ represent the temperature of an object at time $t,$ in minutes, since the moment when it was put in a room. The temperature *decreases* at a rate that is equal to $0.03$ times the difference between the object's temperature and the room's temperature, which is ${25}^{∘}\\text{C}.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nWhich differential equation describes the instantaneous rate of change in the object's temperature?\n\n*Hint: Write the right-hand side of the equation as an expression in terms of $T.$*\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-0.03(T-25)$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nHow fast is the object's temperature *decreasing* at the moment when its temperature is ${60}^{∘}C?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The object's temperature is decreasing at a rate of $-$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1.05$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$degrees per minute.\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nFind $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}T}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}$ in terms of $T.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}T}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0.0009(T-25)$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nFind the general solution to the differential equation in part 1 for $T\\ne 25.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $T\\,=\\,25+K\\cdot \\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e^{-0.03t}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\,$where $K$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nCurrent part:\n\nLet $\\overset{^}{T}(t)$ be a particular solution of the differential equation from the previous part that satisfies the condition $\\overset{^}{T}(0)=28.$ Find $\\overset{^}{T}(t).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\overset{^}{T}(t)\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Previously, we found that the general solution is\n\n$$\nT=25+K{e}^{-0.03t}.\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $T(0)=28$ to find $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}28 & =25+K\\,{e}^{-0.03(0)} \\\\ 28 & =25+K\\,{e}^{0} \\\\ K & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the required particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{T}(t)=25+3{e}^{-0.03t}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295784 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6835b52b-adaa-5c66-9f6d-d32591864659" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Infinite Limits Using the Laws of Exponents and the Algebra of Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb7d16e7-5187-5ceb-aa8a-e7a9cc09d04f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb7d16e7-5187-5ceb-aa8a-e7a9cc09d04f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295786} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcdaa0f5-e40e-5193-a780-3e5319483f83" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4d955e6d-18ef-5aa3-bd70-fde98841cdc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-168079" :question/problem "Let $T(t)$ represent the temperature of an object at time $t,$ in minutes, since the moment when it was put in a room. The temperature *decreases* at a rate that is equal to $0.03$ times the difference between the object's temperature and the room's temperature, which is ${25}^{∘}\\text{C}.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nWhich differential equation describes the instantaneous rate of change in the object's temperature?\n\n*Hint: Write the right-hand side of the equation as an expression in terms of $T.$*\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-0.03(T-25)$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nHow fast is the object's temperature *decreasing* at the moment when its temperature is ${60}^{∘}C?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The object's temperature is decreasing at a rate of $-$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1.05$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$degrees per minute.\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nFind $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}T}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}$ in terms of $T.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}T}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0.0009(T-25)$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nFind the general solution to the differential equation in part 1 for $T\\ne 25.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $T\\,=\\,25+K\\cdot \\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e^{-0.03t}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\,$where $K$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nLet $\\overset{^}{T}(t)$ be a particular solution of the differential equation from the previous part that satisfies the condition $\\overset{^}{T}(0)=28.$ Find $\\overset{^}{T}(t).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\overset{^}{T}(t)\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $25+3e^{-0.03t}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFor the function $\\overset{^}{T}(t)$ from the previous part, what is ${lim}_{t\\to \\,-\\infty }\\overset{^}{T}(t)?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{t\\to \\,-\\infty }\\overset{^}{T}(t)\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the previous part, we know that\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{T}(t)=25+3{e}^{-0.03t}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the laws of exponents and the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to \\,-\\infty }\\overset{^}{T}(t) & ={lim}_{t\\to \\,-\\infty }(25+3{e}^{-0.03t}) \\\\  & =25+3{lim}_{t\\to \\,-\\infty }{e}^{-0.03t} \\\\  & =25+3\\,{e}^{\\,\\,{lim}_{t\\to \\,-\\infty }(-0.03t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{\\,\\,{lim}_{t\\to \\,-\\infty }(-0.03t)}=\\infty ,$ and the positive factor of $3$ does not affect the sign of the limit, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to -\\,\\infty }\\overset{^}{T}(t)=\\infty .\n$$"}]}
